---
name: babysit
description: Use when a pull request needs review-thread triage, CI repair, conflict resolution, merge monitoring, an explicitly requested merge, land, or ship, or terminal cleanup.
---

# Pull request babysit

Get this PR to a merge-ready state. Run the verification commands below, fix what is in scope, and loop until every ruleset requirement is satisfied. Babysit alone stops at merge-ready. Merge, terminal cleanup, and unresolved handoff run only when the sections below say to.

Follow {{.Rule "git"}} for fetches, branch comparisons, signed commits, worktree safety, and the merge contract. Use {{.Skill "cleanup-git"}} for plain Git branch and worktree cleanup. Use {{.Skill "graphite"}} only for a confirmed dependent pull request stack. On a Graphite stack, run this full loop on every open PR, including step 2.

## 1. Identify the PR and read merge state

Derive `owner/repo` from `gh repo view --json nameWithOwner` or from the PR JSON.

```bash
gh pr view --json number,headRefName,baseRefName,mergeable,mergeStateStatus,reviewDecision,statusCheckRollup,isDraft
```

If `isDraft` is `true`, mark the PR ready for review before anything else. A draft PR cannot be merged.

```bash
gh pr ready
```

A draft also shows `mergeStateStatus` of `DRAFT`. A `gh pr ready` run lets the real merge state surface. Automated reviewers like Copilot and Bugbot do not comment on draft PRs by default. That ready state is what triggers the review comments this loop then triages.

## 2. Read the merge contract before a CI wait

Follow {{.Rule "git"}} and read the active ruleset for the PR base branch before a CI wait. Required review threads, approvals, code-owner review, last-push approval, draft state, and conflicts block merge independently of check runs. Fix those during the first CI run. A wait on CI first, then a later non-check blocker, starts another full run.

`gh pr checks` and `statusCheckRollup` are not the ruleset. Compare them to the required check names after the ruleset read.

`mergeStateStatus` of `BLOCKED` is a summary of those ruleset gates. It is not a request for `--admin`.

## 3. Resolve merge conflicts

Resolve merge conflicts while preserving intent on both the PR branch and the base branch. If intents truly conflict, abort the merge and ask the user for clarification before continuing.

If `mergeStateStatus` is `BEHIND`, update the branch from base before re-checking CI. Another PR may have already fixed a failing check. Follow {{.Rule "git"}} for safe base and worktree handling. For a dependent Graphite stack, follow {{.Skill "graphite"}} for restacking and submission.

## 4. Triage inline review comments

Inline diff comments are separate from PR timeline comments.

List inline comments. The PR number is not a `gh api` placeholder, so substitute it from `gh pr view`:

```bash
NUM=$(gh pr view --json number --jq .number)
gh api "repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/$NUM/comments"
```

Read thread resolution state and stable thread IDs with GraphQL:

```graphql
{
  repository(owner: "OWNER", name: "REPO") {
    pullRequest(number: PR_NUMBER) {
      reviewThreads(first: 100) {
        nodes {
          id
          isResolved
          isOutdated
          path
          line
          comments(first: 10) {
            nodes { body author { login } }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Filter to unresolved threads first. Read only each comment body and the minimum location needed to act. Do not dump the full JSON payload.

Automated reviewers (Copilot, Bugbot) re-comment on every push. Thread triage is recurring, not one-time. Each new push can add threads that block merge while `required_review_thread_resolution` is active. Re-read unresolved threads on every babysit loop.

Do not delay a verified review fix to avoid restarting CI or triggering another automated review pass. A local fix that has not been published cannot receive CI or reviewer feedback. Prompt publication is part of the babysit loop. Use the repository-specific push command from local instructions when publishing.

Validate automated review bot comments (for example Bugbot) before acting. Fix valid points. Reply and leave the thread open when the bot is wrong or the concern is unsure.

## 5. Resolve threads once addressed

After a requested change is made, locally verified, and pushed, resolve the thread with GraphQL. REST has no resolve endpoint. Use the `PRRT_...` thread `id` from `reviewThreads`, not a comment ID.

Reply first when a short explanation helps:

```graphql
mutation {
  addPullRequestReviewThreadReply(input: {
    pullRequestReviewThreadId: "PRRT_..."
    body: "Fixed in <commit>."
  }) { comment { id } }
}
```

Then resolve:

```graphql
mutation {
  resolveReviewThread(input: {threadId: "PRRT_..."}) {
    thread { id isResolved }
  }
}
```

Do not ask the user for approval before resolving a thread after its concern is genuinely fixed or superseded. Resolve it autonomously after verifying the current code and relevant checks. When `required_review_thread_resolution` is active in the ruleset, every required thread must be resolved before merge.

Treat every review state read before a reply or thread resolution as stale. Record the action time, then poll the pull request until each automated reviewer affected by that action publishes a completion signal after it. A check run must enter a terminal state. A newer review or edited summary counts only when it states the completed refresh result. Re-read reviews with their submission times, `reviewDecision`, review threads, checks, `mergeable`, and `mergeStateStatus` after that signal. A blocking review submitted before the latest reply or resolution does not prove that dismissal is required. Do not dismiss it, ask the user to dismiss it, or merge around it while the reviewer refresh is still pending. If the reviewer exposes no completion signal, keep the pull request in the babysit loop and report that the review refresh is pending.

## 6. Fix CI within PR scope

Fix CI failures caused by changes within this PR's scope. Publish each scoped CI or review-thread fix as soon as it is locally verified, then re-run `gh pr checks`. Use the repository-specific push workflow from local instructions. Include every unresolved thread already present in the same push as any CI fix. Do not wait for comments that have not been posted yet.

Never edit CI workflows or unrelated code just to make failures pass. Report back when that would be required.

## 7. Loop until merge-ready

After each push, reply, or thread resolution, re-run steps 1 through 6 until the PR is merge-ready. Merge-ready means all of the following hold:

- Required status checks are green.
- `reviewDecision` satisfies the ruleset approval count and code-owner requirements.
- Required review threads are resolved when `required_review_thread_resolution` is active (re-check each loop).
- `mergeable` is not blocked by conflicts.

When merge-ready, stop and report that the PR is ready to merge: required checks green, review decision satisfied, and required threads resolved. When not merge-ready yet, report checks, review decision, unresolved threads, and what still blocks merge. Do not arm auto-merge or merge unless the user explicitly asks (see section 8).

When the session is ending and the PR is not merge-ready or terminal, run section 10 before stopping.

## 8. Merge when the user asks

Run this section only when the user explicitly asks to merge (for example "merge", "land", "ship", or "babysit and merge").

Section 7 requirements are satisfied, or only CI time remains with no real failure and threads resolved.

### Admin bypass authorization

Treat `admin merge` or `admin bypass` as a request for confirmation, not authorization. Ask through the native harness question tool required by {{.Skill "ask-questions"}}. Use `--admin` only after the user confirms that question for the current merge. Never infer the request from "merge", "land", "ship", urgency, available administrator rights, prior turns, or prior pull requests. If the native question tool is unavailable or the user does not confirm, do not use `--admin`. The confirmation expires after that merge attempt.

Ask for an admin merge only when remaining in-scope work is empty and the leftover gate is a bypass the user must confirm.

### Merge paths

A dependent Graphite stack follows {{.Skill "graphite"}} task **Merge a Graphite stack**. That skill owns stack order, merge-when-ready, dry-run behavior, and Graphite commands.

One pull request uses `gh pr merge --merge` or `--squash` per repository policy when fully green. Use `gh pr merge --auto` only when the sole blocker is CI time.

A confirmed admin merge uses `gh pr merge --admin` with the repository's allowed merge method only after the request and confirmation above.

Loop `gh pr view --json state` until `state` is `MERGED` or superseded `CLOSED` (see section 9). When auto-merge is armed, keep triaging new review threads on each loop.

When merge completes or the PR is superseded, run section 9.

## 9. Clean up after merged, superseded, or when the user asks

Run after section 8 completes, when babysit discovers the PR is already terminal, or when the user explicitly asks to clean up (even if the PR is still open).

Cleanup runs for `MERGED` (normal land). Cleanup also runs for `CLOSED` with no `mergedAt` when the work was superseded (another PR landed the same changes, or the user closed it after confirming the branch is obsolete). Cleanup also runs when the user explicitly asks to clean up branches or the worktree (for example "clean up", "remove the worktree", "done here").

Detect terminal PR state with:

```bash
gh pr view --json state,mergedAt,headRefName,baseRefName
git worktree list
```

Record `headRefName`, feature worktree path (if any), PR `state`, and which checkout owns trunk (`git worktree list`).

For a plain Git pull request, invoke {{.Skill "cleanup-git"}} with the pull request branch, its local and remote refs, and its worktree as the bounded candidate set. The skill discovers each blocking path with `git status --porcelain --ignored` and adds it to that bounded set. It classifies ignored paths instead of treating a nonempty status as a keep verdict. It preserves the remote branch while the pull request is open unless the user explicitly abandons the pull request.

For Graphite stacks, use the deletion or synchronization path in {{.Skill "graphite"}} instead of deleting tracked branches with plain Git.

Reconcile the trunk worktree from the checkout that owns trunk (never from a feature worktree):

```bash
git -C <trunk-worktree> fetch origin
git -C <trunk-worktree> merge --ff-only origin/<trunk>
```

Do not `git update-ref refs/heads/<trunk>` when trunk is checked out elsewhere.

For Graphite stacks, follow {{.Skill "graphite"}} for stack cleanup order, then reconcile trunk once from its owning worktree.

Report: PR URL(s), branches deleted (remote/local), trunk commit after reconcile, worktree removed (or skipped and why).

## 10. Session end with no resolution

Run when the babysit session is ending and the PR is not terminal: not `MERGED`, not superseded `CLOSED`, and not merge-ready with a clear path forward. Typical signals: context compaction, the user says they are stopping for now, or an agent turn limit is imminent.

Preserve recoverable state in git and durable memory, then remove the feature worktree. Do not delete the PR branch on remote or local in this path. The work is still in flight.

Capture git state in the feature worktree:

- If the tree is dirty, commit WIP on the PR branch with a message like `babysit handoff WIP` (stage only in-scope files; no `git add .`).
- Create a backup ref under `refs/backup/` only (never `refs/heads/`), matching split-to-prs and graphite conventions:

```bash
git update-ref "refs/backup/babysit-$(git branch --show-current | tr / -)-$(date +%s)" HEAD
```

- Push the PR branch and the backup ref to `origin` when the branch has not been pushed or the backup should survive locally-only loss:

```bash
git push -u origin HEAD
git push origin "refs/backup/babysit-..."
```

Record durable memory so a future session can resume without relying on the compaction summary. Write one handoff block with: repo, PR number and URL, branch name, worktree path, last known blockers (CI, threads, conflicts), backup ref name, and the next concrete babysit step.

| Host capability | Action |
| --- | --- |
| Cursor (`cursor_dialog` available) | Upsert a user rule titled `Babysit handoff: <owner/repo> PR #<n>` with the handoff block as `content`. List existing rules first; `update` when the title matches, otherwise `add`. |
| Cursor memory unavailable | State once that durable memory could not be saved; point the user at the backup ref and PR URL for manual recovery. |
| Other agents | The pushed branch, backup ref, and clyde transcript (per {{.Skill "reorient"}}) are the durable record; include the handoff block in the final user-visible report. |

Clean up the feature worktree only (branches stay):

```bash
git worktree remove <path>
```

Use `--force` only when the tree is dirty after the WIP commit step failed and the user approved.

Report: PR URL, branch name, backup ref, whether memory was saved, worktree path removed, and the single next step for the next babysit session.
